  PASS  and by more than five times

      ⚠ BOTH ROUTES HERE SOLVE DIRECTLY; part 5 adds GMRES on the
      same matrix.

      ⛔ AN EARLIER VERSION CALLED THIS COMPARISON UNFAIR TO THE MARCH,
      on the grounds that its residual is inter-sphere coupling rather
      than error.  WITHDRAWN.  The target is ONE sphere; the march must
      periodize to answer at all, so periodization is its own machinery
      and its cost is its own.  Both errors are method errors.

      ⚠ NEITHER LADDER CONVERGES MONOTONICALLY.  The voxel route's error
      is the staircase, non-monotonic in n_sub because the volume ratio
      is; the march's combines array coupling, oscillatory in spacing
      because it is an interference, with a lateral discretisation that
      does converge, as ~1/N at fixed period.  Quoting a single rate for
      either on this problem would be wrong.

[4] what the cost is made of
      Foldy-Lax: assemble 81 N_c^2 propagator entries, then a DIRECT
      dense solve of the 9 N_c system -- O((9 N_c)^3).
      March: M depth steps x 4 Runge-Kutta stages, each one dense
      (3N)^3 product for Y A12 Y plus block-sparse linear terms.
      measured time exponent, Foldy-Lax: 2.05  (over 288-2520 unknowns)
      measured time exponent,     march: 2.14  (over 108-768 unknowns)
  PASS  both costs grow, and neither is asymptotic at these sizes
      ⚠ four points at small sizes: these exponents are descriptive,
      not asymptotic, and BLAS is not in its cubic regime here.

[5] GMRES on the sphere's Foldy-Lax system
      the earlier claim that the iterative solver 'cannot hold a
      sphere' was wrong: that limit belongs to the FFT block-Toeplitz
      matvec of the periodic slab, not to iterative solution as such.
      This is the same matrix the direct route uses, solved by GMRES.

      n_sub = 6
         eps   9N_c    cond(A)  GMRES its  vs direct   t_gmres  t_direct
        0.02   1224  1.818e+01         54    1.6e-11     0.029     0.061
        0.10   1224  3.387e+02         81    1.2e-10     0.042     0.062
        0.40   1224  3.148e+03        147    2.3e-10     0.076     0.062
        0.80   1224  5.982e+03        210    1.2e-09     0.112     0.062
  PASS  GMRES solves the sphere system at n_sub=6
  PASS  and its count grows with CONTRAST, not size (n_sub=6)

      n_sub = 8
         eps   9N_c    cond(A)  GMRES its  vs direct   t_gmres  t_direct
        0.02   2520  1.686e+01         54    1.4e-11     0.107     0.452
        0.10   2520  3.104e+02         81    1.4e-10     0.151     0.448
        0.40   2520  2.863e+03        147    2.6e-10     0.263     0.447
        0.80   2520  5.525e+03        219    1.2e-09     0.392     0.449
  PASS  GMRES solves the sphere system at n_sub=8
  PASS  and its count grows with CONTRAST, not size (n_sub=8)

      ⚠ THE ITERATION COUNT IS SET BY THE CONTRAST AND NOT BY N.
      54 -> 210 as eps goes 0.02 -> 0.80 at 1224 unknowns, and 54 -> 219
      at 2520.  Doubling the system changes it by 4%.  So the
      iterative route's advantage GROWS with N -- direct is O(n^3) while
      GMRES is (iterations) x O(n^2) -- and shrinks with contrast.
      Measured here at 2520 unknowns, GMRES takes 0.107 s against 0.452 s direct
      at eps=0.02, and 0.392 s against 0.449 s at eps=0.80, where the
      direct route amortises ONE factorisation over all nine right-hand
      sides while GMRES restarts for each.

      ⚠ THIS IS NOT THE FAST MATVEC -- but that one EXISTS; see part 7.
      The matvec here
      is a dense product against an ASSEMBLED matrix, so it costs O(n^2)
      and the assembly itself costs O(N_c^2) blocks.  The voxel centres
      lie on a regular lattice and P~[m,n] depends only on the
      separation, so the block-Toeplitz FFT matvec of lattice_greens
      applies -- embedding the sphere in its bounding box with zero T
      outside.  That is the version worth comparing against the march,
      and it is not built.

[6] the SAME comparison across frequency
      the head-to-head above is at k_S a = 2.4, which is where the
      march's own gate runs.  That is not a neutral choice, so the
      whole comparison is repeated across the band.

        k_S a   ka_sub    voxel FL       march    ratio
         0.50    0.083  8.3811e-03  6.0019e-03      1.4
         1.00    0.167  2.1243e-02  4.2383e-03      5.0
         1.50    0.250  4.5912e-02  1.3120e-02      3.5
         2.40    0.400  1.0729e-01  1.1176e-02      9.6

      voxel error grows as (k_S a)^1.6
      march error varies by only 3.1x over the same band
  PASS  the voxel route's error grows with frequency
  PASS  the march's does not

      ⚠ SO THE HEADLINE RATIO IS NOT A CONSTANT.  It is 1.4x at
      k_S a = 0.5 and 9.6x at 2.4, because the two errors are
      different KINDS of thing: the march's is the array's coupling, a property of
      the arrangement and nearly flat in frequency, while the voxel
      route's is the staircase, whose phase error scales with the cell
      size in wavelengths.  Extrapolating down, they meet near the
      Rayleigh regime; the full sweep (--dump-backscatter) locates the
      crossover.

      ⚠ AND THE FLOOR IS NOT THE SUB-CELL VALIDITY LIMIT.  At k_S a = 2.4
      with n_sub = 6 the sub-cells sit at k a = 0.40, an obvious suspect.
      Refining to n_sub = 10 brings them to 0.24, and on 25 September 2026
      (the package cube T-matrix as the single site) the error was 0.293
      against 0.205 at n_sub = 4: it did not help.  Not repeated here; the
      staircase, not the single site, sets the voxel route's level on
      this sphere (scripts/gate_sphere_cell_average_vs_mie.py).

